+# OPUS-UPDATE-ROOT-R10 — retained ref names the exact pre-rebase tip; printed repair and runbook are one source
+
+Scope: `UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md`, `tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py`, `tools/arifi-sync/test_arifi_sync.py`,
+this report. Nothing staged, nothing committed, no fetch/pull/merge/rebase/push/tag against the real
+repository; every Git operation in the tests happens in throwaway temporary repositories.
+
+## R9 finding 1 — a stale `arifi-pre-rebase` could satisfy this cycle's retention gate
+
+### What was wrong
+
+The gate asked only *does the name resolve to a commit?*
+
+```bash
+git -C <repo> tag arifi-pre-rebase arifi/main   # may already exist from an earlier partial run
+git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet arifi-pre-rebase^{commit} >/dev/null || exit $?
+```
+
+With a tag left behind by an **earlier** cycle, `git tag` fails (name taken, failure deliberately
+not propagated), `rev-parse` succeeds on the stale object, and the rebase then rewrites this cycle's
+tip with nothing retaining it — the one preservation failure the runbook itself says cannot be
+repaired afterwards.
+
+### The fix: the retained ref is named after the object it retains
+
+`retained_ref_name(tip)` → `arifi-pre-rebase-<40-hex tip>`.
+
+Why a derived name rather than fail-closed on a fixed name: with one fixed name the only repair for
+a stale tag is to delete it, and deleting it breaks the reachability check on **every prior cycle's**
+ledger rows, which name that ref (`replay_provenance()` re-reads `retained_ref` per row,
+`arifi_sync.py:2368-2380`). A derived name needs no deletion: another cycle's ref is simply a
+different name, so it can neither answer this gate nor be overwritten to make room for it.
+
+`retention_gate_lines(repo, ref, tip)` is the capture → create-if-absent → verify-exact sequence,
+and it is the ONE source for both the printed repair and the runbook block:
+
+```
+git -C <repo> switch arifi/main || exit $?
+test "$(git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet "arifi/main^{commit}")" = <TIP> || { echo "REFUSE: arifi/main is no longer <TIP> - re-run the bump for the tip you have" >&2; exit 1; }
+git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet "arifi-pre-rebase-<TIP>^{commit}" >/dev/null || git -C <repo> tag arifi-pre-rebase-<TIP> <TIP> || exit $?
+test "$(git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet "arifi-pre-rebase-<TIP>^{commit}")" = <TIP> || { echo "REFUSE: arifi-pre-rebase-<TIP> exists at another object - nothing rewritten, nothing overwritten" >&2; exit 1; }
+git -C <repo> rebase --onto <new-upstream-sha> <old-base-sha> arifi/main || exit $?
+```
+
+Properties, in order of the lines:
+
+| Property | Line that provides it |
+|---|---|
+| exact tip captured BEFORE switch/rebase | `bump` resolves `<TIP>` with `_commit_sha()` at print time, before anything runs |
+| tip is still the tip after the switch | line 2 — if the branch moved since the print, stop before any tag is made |
+| retention created only when absent | line 3 — `tag` runs only if the ref does not already exist, and propagates its own status when it does run |
+| retained ref proved to resolve to the captured SHA before any rewrite | line 4 — `test … = <TIP>`, so a lying `tag`, a missing ref and a wrong object all stop here |
+| collision handled, never silently | line 4 fails closed; `tag -f` appears nowhere |
+| stale ref from another cycle cannot answer | the name embeds `<TIP>`, so an earlier cycle's ref is a different name |
+| unresolvable tip prints no rewrite at all | `bump_repair_sequence()` stops at the one runnable step (re-run the bump) rather than emitting `arifi-pre-rebase-` and a gate comparing against the empty string |
+
+`--retained-ref` downstream now carries the same derived name, in the printed sequence and in the
+runbook's identity block.
+
+## R9 finding 2 — the printed repair and the runbook were not the same sequence
+
+Two defects, both closed:
+
+1. **The runbook had fences the generator did not.** `bump_repair_sequence()` printed bare
+   `switch`/`tag`/`rebase` with no status propagation and no verification. It now emits
+   `retention_gate_lines()` verbatim, and `test_the_runbook_retention_block_IS_the_generated_one`
+   asserts line-for-line that the runbook block **is** `retention_gate_lines("<repo>", "arifi/main",
+   "<pre-rebase-tip-sha>")` plus the rebase line. A fix applied to one and not the other is RED.
+2. **Generated commands did not propagate failure.** Measured, not assumed: the first run of the
+   rewired adversarial harness caught a conflicted `rebase` returning **rc 0** through the generated
+   sequence, because only the runbook copy had ever been given `|| exit $?`. Every gated generated
+   step now carries it — `switch`, the two `test` fences, `tag`, `rebase`, `replay-map`, each
+   `git add`, each `git commit`, `set-base-pin`, `series regen`, and the `bump` re-run — with no
+   ambient `set -e` anywhere in the harness.
+
+`RunbookRebasePrerequisiteBashTest` now executes the **generated** sequence (sliced by content at
+the `rebase --onto` line, so a gate line added later cannot drift out of what is run), not an
+extracted runbook block.
+
+## Exact changes
