+## 1. Finding 1 — dirty source contents were not bound to the receipt
+
+**Repair.** `_tree_identity` now carries `content_sha256`: a sha256 over the actual bytes of every
+path `git status` reports as modified, deleted or untracked, keyed by status code, path and rename
+origin. `_identity_matches` requires it alongside `head`, `tree` and `status_sha256`. Status is read
+with `--porcelain -z --untracked-files=all` and parsed as fields, not lines, so a path containing a
+space cannot mangle the digest and a rename's second NUL field is consumed rather than being read as
+the next entry.
+
+`cmd_build` samples the identity before the compiler and again after it, and refuses to publish a
+receipt when the two differ. The comment at the old line 959 that claimed a mid-build edit could not
+be certified was false; it is true now, and enforced rather than asserted.
+
+The build directory is excluded from the identity by canonical-prefix test, so the build's own
+output — the receipt included — cannot be an input to the identity that certifies it.
+
+**Tests.** `test_dirty_bytes_CHANGING_changes_the_identity` pins the exact shape the review
+reproduced: HEAD, tree and the status *listing* all stay equal while the content hash moves.
+`test_judge_refuses_after_a_dirty_file_changed_to_DIFFERENT_dirty_content` drives it through judge,
+and asserts the must-not-fire half first (unchanged dirty bytes are still judgeable).
+`test_a_staged_rename_does_not_desynchronize_the_content_parse` pins the `-z` two-field rename parse.
+`test_an_edit_DURING_the_build_is_not_certified` mutates a source file from inside the stubbed
+compiler call and asserts the compiler ran, the build returned 1, and no receipt exists.
+
+## 2. Finding 2 — a successful build could certify a different CMake source root
+
+**Repair.** New `build_root_problems`, run in `cmd_build` before anything else and again in
+`cmd_judge`. It reads `CMAKE_HOME_DIRECTORY` from the cache with `_cache_internal`, a raw reader that
+deliberately does *not* drop the INTERNAL class `parse_cache` drops, and compares it to the checkout
+under `_canon` (realpath, normpath, normcase). It also refuses a build directory that is not inside
+the checkout, which covers both an absolute `build_dir` and one that traverses out. An absent
+`CMAKE_HOME_DIRECTORY` is refused, not skipped.
+
+The receipt records `cmake_home`, `build_dir_abs` and `config`, all canonical, and `judge` refuses a
+receipt whose recorded roots are not the ones this run resolves. This is content and canonical-path
+binding, not a directory-name check.
+
+**Tests.** `test_a_cache_naming_another_source_root_refuses_before_the_compiler`,
+`test_a_build_dir_outside_the_checkout_refuses`, `test_a_cache_with_no_source_root_at_all_refuses`,
+`test_judge_refuses_a_receipt_written_for_another_build_root`. The first three assert the compiler
+was never invoked and that no receipt survives.
+
+## 3. Finding 3 — receipts certified no executable bytes and survived failed rebuilds
+
+**Repair, four parts.**
+
+- *Invalidate first.* `cmd_build` deletes any existing receipt before the compiler runs. A refused,
+  failed or interrupted build therefore leaves no certification behind.
+- *Atomic publish.* The receipt is written to `.tmp` and `os.replace`d, so a half-written file can
+  never be read as a certification.
+- *Artifact hashes.* `artifact_digest` records a sha256 of the resolved judge binary and of every
+  shared library in its directory. Scope is the directory of the *resolved* binary, shared through
+  `_judge_binary`, because the candidate list spans `bin/` and `bin/Release/`; a build that scanned
+  one while judge resolved the other would refuse every real run. `.pdb`/`.ilk`/`.exp` are excluded
+  as relink churn.
+- *Verify at judge.* `artifact_problems` requires every recorded artifact to be present and
+  byte-identical, and requires the resolved binary to be one the receipt recorded. An *unrecorded*
+  new file is deliberately not a refusal, so building a second target later does not brick judge.
+  A receipt with no `artifacts` block at all is refused by name.
+
+A build that reports success but produces no `llama-server` is treated as partial and gets no
+receipt.
+
+**Tests.** `test_judge_refuses_a_REPLACED_binary`, `test_judge_refuses_a_replaced_runtime_LIBRARY`,
+`test_judge_refuses_a_PARTIAL_build_that_lost_a_recorded_artifact`,
+`test_judge_refuses_a_receipt_that_records_no_artifacts_at_all`,
+`test_a_FAILED_build_removes_the_previous_receipt`,
+`test_a_rebuild_that_fails_after_a_GOOD_build_leaves_no_certification`,
+`test_an_UNRECORDED_new_file_in_the_build_dir_is_not_a_refusal`, and the end-to-end
+`test_a_successful_build_records_the_artifact_bytes_and_judge_accepts_them`, which runs build then
+judge and stops at the bench-purity gate.
+
+## 4. Finding 4 — the ordinary generated-series candidate could not satisfy the identity gate
+
+This was the finding that made the previous pass non-convergent, and it needed a different shape,
+not a stronger check.
+
+**Repair.**
+
+- *The candidate is retained.* `_replay` gained `keep_branch`, and `bump` uses it. The replay
+  worktree is still destroyed; the commit it produced now survives on `arifi-sync/replay`, so the
+  one tree a bump is actually about can be diffed, inspected and checked out. `_replay` already
+  created and deleted that branch on every run — only its lifetime changed. No canonical branch is
+  created or moved, and nothing is fetched or pulled.
+- *Normalized identity, not raw trees.* `bump` leg 2b requires a clean checkout and an empty
+  `git diff --name-only <replay-tip> HEAD -- . ':(exclude)patches/series'`. Comparing raw trees could
+  never match, because the generator excludes its own directory and the replayed tree therefore
+  carries no series at all.
+- *The exclusion is closed separately.* New `series_integrity_problems` proves that
+  `patches/series` regenerates byte-identically from git at HEAD and that every patch is a committed
+  blob. That is what stops a hand-edited patch hiding inside the excluded directory. It runs no
+  replay, so `bump` does not pay for a second `git am`. `cmd_series_check` was refactored onto the
+  same predicate, so the report and the gate cannot disagree.
+- *The printed repair now converges,* and includes the two steps the review said were missing: move
+  the base pin **before** regenerating (a stale pin re-emits the entire upstream delta as patches),
+  and commit both the pin and the regenerated series.
+
+All `:(exclude)` pathspecs go through `series_pathspec`, which normalizes separators and strips a
+trailing slash. An exclusion that silently matches nothing is a comparison that quietly changed its
+meaning.
+
+**Tests.** `BumpCandidateIdentityTest` no longer stubs `_replay` at all — the replays are real
+`git am` runs on a real miniature fork with a real committed series, on an ordinary upstream-bump
+shape. Only `build`, `judge` and the protected-win preflight (which has its own tests) are replaced.
+`test_the_documented_repair_sequence_CONVERGES_to_a_green_bump` asserts the first `bump` refuses,
+then executes exactly the printed sequence — rebase, move the pin in a *committed* `sources.json`,
+regen, commit — reloads the config **from disk** so the new pin is what drives the second run, and
+asserts the same command now reaches build and judge. `_write_sources` commits the config so the pin
+move is a real repository event, not an in-memory dict edit.
+`test_the_replayed_candidate_is_RETAINED_and_holds_the_bumped_tree` asserts the retained branch
+carries upstream's file and matches the checkout outside the series. The adversarial half:
+`test_bump_refuses_a_HAND_EDITED_patch_the_series_exclusion_would_otherwise_hide`,
+`test_bump_refuses_a_STALE_series_that_no_longer_regenerates`,
+`test_bump_refuses_an_UNCOMMITTED_patch_file`, `test_bump_refuses_a_dirty_checkout`,
+`test_bump_refuses_when_the_checkout_is_still_on_the_old_base`. Every refusal case also asserts
+build and judge never ran.
