+# OPUS-UPDATE-ROOT-R3-REPORT
+
+Maker: Opus 5, 2026-09-12, worktree `C:/ArifiLabs/cache/hq57-update-preservation-wt`, branch
+`codex/hq57-update-preservation`, HEAD `766cb9528` (unchanged — nothing was committed).
+
+Scope honoured: no upstream fetch, no llama executable, no benchmark, no performance or bandwidth
+measurement, no scheduled-task change, no integration, no commit, no edit to any protected manifest
+or to history. Only the four owned paths were written. Every existing provenance, protected-win,
+series-integrity, status and currency gate is still enforced; none was relaxed to make a case pass.
+
+## Changed paths
+
+| Path | Change |
+|---|---|
+| `tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py` | all eight repairs |
+| `tools/arifi-sync/test_arifi_sync.py` | new adversarial coverage, one fixture correction, three fixture-effect corrections |
+| `UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md` | receipt identity contract, complete repair sequence, draft object-store effects, replay-provenance boundary |
+| `OPUS-UPDATE-ROOT-R3-REPORT.md` | this file |
+
+`git status --porcelain` shows exactly these three tracked modifications plus the pre-existing
+untracked review documents. No other tracked file moved.
+
+## Repairs, against the R2 findings
+
+### 1 — in-source build directory (R2 finding 1)
+
+`_strict_descendant` is new and `_under`'s acceptance of equality no longer decides anything about
+build directories. `build_root_problems` refuses when the build directory IS the checkout or
+contains it, and additionally when the directory holds any file tracked at `HEAD` — a build
+directory carrying source would remove that source from the identity exactly as an in-source build
+does. `_status_entries` refuses the same shape at the point of USE, raising rather than honouring a
+`skip_dir` that is not a strict descendant, because `cmd_judge` samples the identity before it
+reaches the configuration check.
+
+The accepted and rejected configurations are exercised end to end in
+`test_the_ACCEPTED_configuration_builds_and_the_REJECTED_one_does_not`: a strict subdirectory
+compiles and publishes a receipt, and the same fixture with `build_dir: "."` makes zero compiler
+calls. `test_excluding_the_checkout_from_the_identity_is_REFUSED_at_the_point_of_use` asserts the
+positive half too — a dirty source file is still visible through a real build directory.
+
+### 2 — the decoy cache (R2 finding 2)
+
+`canonical_cache()` names the one cache the compiler is handed: the `CMakeCache.txt` under
+`build.build_dir`. `cmd_build` passes it to `cmd_recipe_diff` explicitly instead of letting the
+subcommand resolve `build.live_cache`, and `build_root_problems` refuses outright when
+`build.live_cache` canonicalises to a different file — so `cmd_judge`, which calls the same
+predicate, re-checks it as well. `cmd_recipe_diff`'s standalone `--live` override is untouched.
+
+The checker's fixture is reproduced in
+`test_a_DECOY_live_cache_refuses_before_the_compiler_and_leaves_no_receipt`: the snapshot records
+`REQUIRED_FLAG:BOOL=ON`, a decoy cache carries it, the real build cache does not. Asserted: return
+code 1, `c.calls == []`, and no receipt on disk.
+
+### 3 — receipt dropped before every refusal (R2 finding 3)
+
+The drop is unconditional and now precedes the missing-cache path. Ordering was corrected on
+advice: the build-directory SHAPE is judged first, because `_drop_receipt` deletes a file inside
+`build_dir` and a `build_dir` misconfigured onto a source path would otherwise have the function
+delete from the source tree on its way to refusing that configuration. Atomic publication
+(`tmp` + `os.replace`) is unchanged.
+
+One behavioural note. With the root check running first, a build directory whose `CMakeCache.txt`
+is absent is refused by the root check ("states no `CMAKE_HOME_DIRECTORY`") rather than by the
+missing-cache `Loud`. The receipt is dropped on that path, which is the property the review asked
+for; the `Loud` message still exists and still names the reconfigure repair. Both are asserted
+(`test_a_MISSING_cmake_cache_ALSO_drops_the_previous_receipt`,
+`test_the_missing_cache_message_still_names_the_repair`).
+
+### 4 — selected binary and adjacent runtime set (R2 finding 4)
+
+`artifact_digest` hashes the explicitly selected binary unconditionally, whatever its name, so
+`bin/llama-server` is no longer dropped by the suffix filter. `artifact_problems` now refuses an
+ADDED runtime library (`.dll`, `.so`, `.so.N`, `.dylib`) in the judge binary's own directory, while
+an added unrelated executable stays permissible — it is not on a loader path. The receipt records
+`judge_binary`, the selected candidate's relative path, and `cmd_judge` requires the binary it
+resolves to be that one; without it a build that selected the extensionless candidate and a judge
+run that resolves a later-appearing `.exe` would each be internally consistent and be about
+different files.
+
+Five new tests cover: extensionless hashing and judgeability, a replaced extensionless binary, an
+added DLL, an added `.so.1`, and the resolved-versus-recorded binary mismatch. The existing
+"unrecorded new file is not a refusal" test still passes on an added `.exe`.
+
+### 5 — the printed sequence is the tested sequence (R2 finding 6, first half)
+
+`bump_repair_sequence()` is the single source for the refusal's printed repair. It includes
+`git add -- <series dir>` before the pathspec commit, and also the `add` for `sources.json` so the
+sequence is uniform. `cmd_series_check`'s printed repair was corrected the same way.
+
+The hidden-step hole is closed *durably* rather than by transcription. The convergence test captures
+the refusal's stdout, and every git command it runs goes through `_ran`/`_ran_commit`, which assert
+that the exact command line appears in the captured text before executing it. A step dropped from
+the print is now a red test. The old `regen_and_commit` helper is no longer used by the convergence
+path; the staging step is executed explicitly.
+
+The one deliberate, disclosed deviation: the printed commit lines carry no `-m` because they expect
+an editor. The test asserts the printed `git -C <repo> commit -- <pathspec>` verbatim and then runs
+that same pathspec commit with a message and trailer supplied non-interactively. The operation under
+test — a pathspec commit of exactly that path — is asserted literally.
+
+### 6 — replay provenance for rewritten commits (R2 finding 6, second half)
+
+`cmd_protected_win_check` is no longer stubbed anywhere in `BumpCandidateIdentityTest`. Only the two
+hardware legs are replaced.
+
+Unstubbing first required a fixture correction, on advice: `Repo.commit`'s default effect
+("test fixture") states no quantity and matches no `NO_EFFECT` prefix, so `_measured_effect_is_real`
+classifies it as REAL. Every fixture commit was therefore a measured win, which is why R2 stubbed
+the preflight. Effects are explicit now, exactly one commit is a real measured win, and
+`test_the_fixture_effects_are_what_the_classifier_actually_says` pins that so it cannot silently
+regress. `Repo.commit`'s default is unchanged, to avoid rippling into other suites.
+
+The mechanism is a new tracked ledger, `tools/arifi-sync/replay-provenance.json`, written by
+`protected-win replay-map --ref <ours> --retained-ref <tag> --recorded-by <who> --write`. It is in
+`CANDIDATE_FILES`, so a copy that exists only on disk cannot certify a ref.
+
+The generator cannot register anything. Candidate originals are drawn only from commits already in
+`protected-wins.json` or the baseline, and only where they are contained in the retained ref. A
+rebased measured-effect commit is mapped only when exactly one candidate has the same patch id,
+the same trailers and the same subject. Zero matches or more than one are named, printed, and the
+command exits non-zero without writing that row.
+
+`replay_provenance()` re-derives every row at use time and trusts nothing recorded. Per row:
+required fields present; both shas are commits; the mapping is one-to-one across the WHOLE ledger;
+`retained_ref` resolves and contains the original; live `git patch-id --stable` on both sides is
+non-empty, equal, and equal to the recorded value; trailers correspond to each other and to the
+record; subjects likewise; and the original is itself registered or baselined. A failing row maps
+nothing, so its commit is still reported as UNREGISTERED and the manual action is named twice.
+Strict registration, discovery, manifest validation and the baseline ancestry boundary are all
+untouched and all still evaluated against the current ref. No SHA is ever accepted because its
+message matches.
+
+One real bug was found by the new tests and fixed: ambiguity originally poisoned only the second of
+two conflicting rows, letting the first through. One-to-one is now decided over the whole ledger
+before any row is accepted, and both sides of every duplicate are dropped.
+
+Coverage: `test_the_documented_repair_sequence_CONVERGES_to_a_green_bump` drives a real registered
+measured win end to end — retain, rebase, `replay-map --write`, stage and commit the ledger, move
+the pin, regenerate, stage and commit the series, then a second `bump` with the preflight unstubbed
+reaching legs 3 and 4. It asserts the win's sha really was rewritten and that the mapping is what
+carries the registration. `test_without_the_replay_ledger_the_rebased_win_REFUSES_as_unregistered`
+is the paired RED and reproduces the R2 failure exactly. Five more REDs cover a non-equivalent
+patch, an ambiguous ledger, an unregistered original, a missing retained ref, and a hand-edited
+patch id; two more cover the generator refusing to guess and the on-disk-only ledger.
+
+### 7 — draft snapshot (R2 finding 7)
+
+`draft_snapshot` refuses by default on any untracked, non-ignored file, and the refusal is taken
+before a single Git object is written. Tracked modifications and deletions are staged with
+`git add -u`, which cannot add a new path; `git add -A` is gone from this path entirely. A new
+candidate file is included only through `--allow-new <path>`, one flag per path, each of which must
+exist, be untracked, and be under `DRAFT_MAX_NEW_BYTES` (1 MiB). Nothing is included by pattern,
+directory or size alone.
+
+Nine tests: sensitive untracked content refused with its bytes proven absent from the object store
+(`git hash-object` without `-w`, then `git cat-file -e` must fail); an ignored file neither refused
+nor included; an explicitly allowed new file included; an allowance that does not open the door to
+the rest of the untracked set; an oversize allowance refused; an allowance naming a tracked file
+refused; a partially staged tree whose real index bytes, refs, `HEAD`, worktree bytes and porcelain
+status are all byte-identical afterwards; a tracked deletion reaching the snapshot; and the
+subcommand naming `--allow-new` in its refusal.
+
+**Order-dependent flake in the partially-staged test, diagnosed and repaired.** In the R3 full-suite
+run this test failed with `the real index was modified`, and it passed when run alone or as its own
+class. The cause was in the test, not in `draft_snapshot`. The index bytes were captured FIRST and
+the `git status --porcelain` baseline was taken AFTER, so the comparison ran against bytes git
+itself had already rewritten: `git status` refreshes the index stat cache and writes it back, and a
+racily-clean entry - one whose mtime falls in the same granularity window as the index write - has
+its `size` field zeroed on that write. That is exactly what the failure showed, `\x03` (the three
+bytes of a staged `a2\n`) becoming `\x00`, with the trailer checksum following. The apparent
+423-versus-427 length difference in the assertion message is `unittest`'s elided *repr* length, not
+a byte-length difference; a printable byte costs one repr character and a non-printable four.
+
+Measured before editing, 40 iterations of the exact fixture with the real index hashed at each step:
+
+| Step | Iterations where the real index changed |
+|---|---|
+| the test's own baseline `git status --porcelain` | 12 of 40 |
+| `draft_snapshot` | 0 of 40 |
+
+`git ls-files --stage` was identical throughout all 40, in both the drifting and the quiet runs. So
+the snapshot never touched the operator's index, and the assertion was reading a moving baseline.
+
+The repair moves the index read to be the LAST baseline capture, immediately before the snapshot
+call, so the only writer that can run between capture and assertion is `draft_snapshot` itself. The
+byte-level assertion is kept exactly as it was - it is strictly stronger than a staging comparison,
+and with the ordering fixed it is deterministic. A comment on the capture states why it has to stay
+last. No production code was changed for this, and no preflight or convergence behaviour is
+involved: the edit is the ordering of two capture lines inside one test.
+
+### 8 — the operator runbook
+
+`UPDATE-RUNBOOK.md` §2.1 now carries an explicit identity contract, in two tables: what a receipt
+BINDS (committed source, uncommitted source bytes, CMake source root, build directory shape,
+recipe entries from the canonical cache, `Release`, the selected judge binary including
+extensionless, and the adjacent runtime-library set) and what it does NOT (compiler/linker/SDK/
+driver, build environment and `CMAKE_*` variables, `INTERNAL`/`STATIC` cache entries, libraries
+elsewhere on the loader path, git-ignored files inside the checkout, submodule contents, everything
+in the build directory except the hashed artifacts, and anything after the receipt is written). It
+states in terms that this is neither a hermetic nor a reproducible-build claim.
+
+Also documented: the complete materialisation sequence with `git add -- patches/series` and why it
+is not optional; `--draft`'s object-store effects, the untracked refusal, and explicit new-file
+authorisation; and the replay-provenance boundary below.
+
+## Identity limitations and residuals
+
+1. **Context-shifted replays cannot be mapped automatically, and this is the common case.**
+   `git patch-id --stable` hashes context lines. Measured on this box 2026-09-12 with a throwaway
+   fixture: upstream edits the lines adjacent to ours, the rebase applies with no conflict, and the
+   patch id still differs. `replay-map` reports the commit UNMATCHED and requires manual
+   registration. Left fail-closed deliberately — loosening it to subject or fuzzy matching is the
+   laundering the mandate forbids. Pinned by `ReplayPatchIdLimitTest`, both the disjoint case that
+   maps and the adjacent case that does not.
+2. **Replay provenance depends on retained objects.** A row is only checkable while the original is
+   reachable from the named ref. A fresh clone without that ref cannot verify the ledger, and the
+   commits will read as unregistered. The runbook instructs retaining the pre-rebase tip as a real
+   tag before rewriting.
+3. **The receipt binds no toolchain, environment, loader path or submodule content.** Stated in full
+   in the runbook table. This is a limitation of the receipt, not of these repairs.
+4. **The recipe diff is not a complete effective configuration.** `parse_cache` drops `INTERNAL` and
+   `STATIC` entries by design; only `CMAKE_HOME_DIRECTORY` is read back out of that class.
+5. **Ignored files inside the checkout are outside the source identity**, so an ignored generated
+   input the build reads is invisible to it.
+6. **`_patch_id` is not evaluated for merge commits.** The series is required to be linear, so this
+   has no path to fire today; a merge would yield an empty patch id and refuse, which is correct.
+7. **No hardware was exercised.** No cmake, no llama-server, no GPU, no measurement. The build and
+   judge legs remain as untested on this box as before.
+8. **Fixed in passing, disclosed:** `cmd_scope --json` referenced an unimported `Path` and raised
+   `NameError` on the only line that writes its output. It now uses `open()`. Disclosed by the R2
+   maker and re-disclosed by the R2 checker; it is in a file I own, so shipping R3 with it
+   known-broken was not defensible.
+
