+## 1. The blocker — a printed line that a shell could not run, and a test that did not run it
+
+**What was wrong.** `bump_repair_sequence()` printed
+
+```
+    python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py protected-win replay-map --ref main \
+        --retained-ref arifi-pre-rebase --recorded-by <you> --write
+```
+
+in a block whose banner said every indented line is a command to run as printed. `<you>` is not a
+placeholder to a shell: it is input redirection, so the line reads a file named `you` and never
+supplies the required value. The convergence test then side-stepped that line entirely by calling
+the private `cmd_protected_win_replay_map()` through `_replay_map()` — so the one command that was
+not executable as printed was also the one command the test did not execute.
+
+**Why an expansion is not the fix.** `--recorded-by "$(git config user.email)"` is executable in a
+shell, but the convergence test runs the printed line through `shlex.split(posix=True)` and
+`main` — there is no shell — so `$(git config user.email)` would be recorded verbatim as the
+operator in `replay-provenance.json`. The only form that is both *executable as printed* and
+*executed as printed* is a concrete value resolved when the line is printed.
+
+**The fix.** `recorded_by_identity(repo)` (`arifi_sync.py`, above `bump_repair_sequence`) resolves
+the identity from the checkout that is being repaired: `git config --get user.email`, then
+`user.name`, then the OS user, then the constant `unknown-operator`. Every candidate is validated
+against `^[A-Za-z0-9._@+ -]+$` before it is accepted, so a value carrying `<`, `>`, `"`, `'`, `\`,
+`$`, `%` or a backtick — `Someone <someone@host>` is an ordinary git ident and is redirection in a
+shell — falls through to the next source instead of being printed. `_shell_word()` emits the value
+bare when it needs no quoting and in **double** quotes when it does: single quotes (what
+`shlex.quote` would emit) do not group in cmd.exe, and this block is pasted on Windows as often as
+in bash; double quotes group in bash, PowerShell and cmd, and `shlex.split(posix=True)` strips them.
+
+`--recorded-by` stays `required=True` in the parser. Nothing about the registration statement is
+weakened; the flag is filled with the operator the checkout already names, and two printed `#`
+comment lines say where the value came from and to change it when recording for somebody else.
+
+**The banner no longer overclaims.** Deleting `<you>` would have left the block asserting verbatim
+execution of the `git commit -- <path>` lines, which the test does not do — it asserts each of them
+verbatim and then supplies the message non-interactively, because an editor cannot open in a test.
+The banner now states exactly that, and says there is no placeholder left to fill in.
+
+**The convergence test executes the public printed command.** `_materialise()` step 2 is
+`self._run_printed("replay-map")`: the printed line is located in the captured refusal, split, and
+handed to `A.main(argv)`. No private helper is substituted for it. `_replay_map()` survives with
+exactly **one** caller — `test_replay_map_REFUSES_to_guess_when_nothing_matches`
+(`test_arifi_sync.py:2169`), which builds an adversarial state the printed sequence cannot produce —
+and `_materialise()` no longer calls it. `_write_sources_file` remains deleted.
