+```bash
+safe='^[A-Za-z0-9._@+ -]+$'
+names_nobody='^(unknown([ ._-]?operator)?|operator|someone|somebody|anonymous|nobody|none|n/?a|tbd|you|your[ ._-]?(name|address)|changeme|[^@]*@example\.(com|org|net|invalid))$'
+```
+
+and each candidate must pass both halves before it is selected:
+
+```bash
+if printf '%s' "$candidate" | grep -Eq "$safe" &&
+   ! printf '%s' "$candidate" | grep -Eiq "$names_nobody"; then
+```
+
+These mirror `arifi_sync.py`'s `_RECORDED_BY_SAFE` (`arifi_sync.py:2829`) and
+`_RECORDED_BY_PLACEHOLDER` (`arifi_sync.py:2839`), the same pair used by `recorded_by_problem()`
+(`arifi_sync.py:2886`) for `replay-map --write` and for every strict provenance read. The placeholder
+rule is case-insensitive **in the flag** (`re.IGNORECASE` / `grep -Ei`) and the pattern stays
+POSIX-ERE-compatible, so no inline `(?i)` can leak into a regex `grep -E` would read literally. The
+tool-side constants carry a comment stating that the runbook mirrors them, and the mirror is pinned
+by a test (§3) rather than by convention. A placeholder is a **fall-through**, not a hard stop: a
+checkout whose `user.email` names nobody but whose `user.name` names a real person still proceeds,
+exactly as on the Python rung.
