+### 2.1a Materialising the candidate — the sequence that makes `bump` converge
+
+A first `bump` on an unrebased checkout always refuses at leg 2b, and that refusal is correct: the
+series replayed onto the new base in a worktree, and your checkout is still on the old one.
+
+The identity `bump` requires is **not** raw tree equality, and must not be — the generated series
+excludes its own directory, so the replayed tree carries no `patches/series` at all while your
+checkout carries the committed one. Two things are checked instead, and together they are the whole
+tree with no gate dropped:
+
+1. the checkout is clean and **identical to the replayed candidate everywhere outside**
+   `patches/series`; and
+2. `patches/series` itself regenerates byte-identically from git and every patch is a committed
+   blob — so the one directory excluded from the comparison is proved separately, and a hand-edited
+   patch cannot hide inside the exclusion.
+
+Run these yourself, in this order. `bump` prints the same list and executes none of it; it never
+fetches, never pulls and never moves a branch.
+
+**Retain the pre-rebase tip BEFORE you rewrite anything.** A rebase gives every replayed commit a
+new sha, and a registered protected win's manifest entry names the OLD one. Without a retained ref
+the originals become unreachable and the registration cannot be carried across the rewrite — the
+preservation preflight then refuses the update as an unregistered measured win, which is correct
+and which you cannot repair afterwards without the objects.
+
+`<pre-rebase-tip-sha>` below is the tip you are about to rewrite, captured BEFORE the switch:
+`git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify "arifi/main^{commit}"`. `bump` prints this block with that sha
+already resolved into it, so you normally paste it rather than fill it in.
+
+```bash
+git -C <repo> switch arifi/main || exit $?
+test "$(git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet "arifi/main^{commit}")" = <pre-rebase-tip-sha> || { echo "REFUSE: arifi/main is no longer <pre-rebase-tip-sha> - re-run the bump for the tip you have" >&2; exit 1; }
+git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet "arifi-pre-rebase-<pre-rebase-tip-sha>^{commit}" >/dev/null || git -C <repo> tag arifi-pre-rebase-<pre-rebase-tip-sha> <pre-rebase-tip-sha> || exit $?
+test "$(git -C <repo> rev-parse --verify --quiet "arifi-pre-rebase-<pre-rebase-tip-sha>^{commit}")" = <pre-rebase-tip-sha> || { echo "REFUSE: arifi-pre-rebase-<pre-rebase-tip-sha> exists at another object - nothing rewritten, nothing overwritten" >&2; exit 1; }
+git -C <repo> rebase --onto <new-upstream-sha> <old-base-sha> arifi/main || exit $?
+```
+
+`switch` and `rebase` carry `|| exit $?` exactly like every command below them, and for the same
+reason: a `switch` that refuses — a stale lane worktree holding the branch, §5.2 — leaves you on the
+wrong checkout, so the tag would name the wrong tip; a `rebase` that stops on a conflict leaves the
+series only half replayed, and `replay-map` would then map a line that does not exist. Neither
+failure may reach the ledger, the pin or a commit.
+
+**The retained ref is named after the tip it retains.** A single fixed `arifi-pre-rebase` could be
+satisfied by a stale tag an EARLIER cycle left behind: `git tag` fails because the name is taken, a
+"does it resolve?" check passes on the stale object, and the rebase then rewrites THIS cycle's tip
+with nothing retaining it — the unrepairable case above, reached through the fence meant to prevent
+it. Deleting the stale tag is not the repair either: every prior cycle's ledger rows name that ref
+and their reachability check reads it. So the name carries the sha, another cycle's ref is simply a
+different name, and no ref is ever overwritten to make room.
+
+`git tag` does not always run, and its being skipped is not a failure. When THIS cycle's exact name
+is already there from a partial run of THIS cycle, the create-if-absent guard skips the creation and
+the sequence continues over a tip that is already retained. An EARLIER cycle's stale ref is simply a
+different name: it neither answers this gate nor stops it, so this cycle's name is still created,
+still verified against the captured tip, and the sequence continues — leaving the stale ref, and the
+old fixed `arifi-pre-rebase`, exactly where they were. `tag` therefore runs only when the name does
+not resolve at all, and when it then refuses — whatever the cause — that status is the sequence's
+status, and nothing below it runs. The `test` line after `tag` is what makes the skip safe rather than a
+swallowed failure: the ref must resolve to exactly `<pre-rebase-tip-sha>` before anything is
