+```bash
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py protected-win validate --draft
+```
+
+`--draft` snapshots your working tree into a throwaway commit — a real, nameable object with `HEAD`
+as its parent — and validates **that**. Nothing is committed, no ref is created, and no branch
+moves. Every gate runs unmodified against the snapshot, so a draft with a
+stale anchor, a missing field or an unregistered measured win still fails exactly as it would after
+committing. `--draft` cannot be combined with a `--ref` naming anything other than `HEAD`: a draft
+is the tree in front of you, and using it to certify some other ref is the wrong-tree trap wearing a
+new flag. Commit the entry, then run the normal `validate --ref <the ref>`.
+
+**`--draft` writes Git objects, and that is why it refuses untracked files.** "Nothing is
+committed" is not "nothing is written." The snapshot IS a commit object: its tree and every blob in
+it go into `.git/objects` and stay there, unreachable but present, until a `gc` that may never run.
+Sweeping in whatever else happens to be lying in the checkout would therefore store unrelated
+content — a key, a dump, a customer file — durably and silently. So:
+
