+```bash
+python tools/arifi-sync/arifi_sync.py protected-win validate --draft \
+    --allow-new tools/arifi-sync/protected-wins.json
+```
+
+Nothing is included by pattern, by directory or by size alone. If the refusal lists a path you did
+not expect, look at it before you authorise it — that is the entire point of the refusal.
+
+**Replay provenance and its boundary — `replay-map`.** A rebase rewrites every sha on the line, and
+a registered win's manifest entry names the old one. `tools/arifi-sync/replay-provenance.json`
+carries that registration across the rewrite, one row per commit, and each row is re-derived from
+scratch every time it is read: the original must still be registered (or baselined), still be
+reachable from the retained ref the row names, and carry an equivalent patch, the same trailers and
+the same subject. A row that fails any of those maps nothing, and its commit is then reported as an
+unregistered measured win — the ledger can never quiet the gate by existing. It is a tracked file
+of the tree it describes; a copy that lives only on disk is refused by the same wrong-tree guard as
+the manifests.
+
+**The boundary, measured on this box 2026-09-12 and left fail-closed on purpose:** equivalence is
+`git patch-id --stable`, which ignores line numbers, whitespace and blob hashes but **hashes context
+lines**. So when upstream has edited the lines *next to* one of our patches, the rebase applies
+cleanly and the patch id still differs. `replay-map` reports that commit as UNMATCHED and refuses to
+write a row for it; you must register it by hand in `protected-wins.json`. Expect this on any bump
+where upstream worked in the same neighbourhood as a registered win — it is the common case, not the
+exotic one. It is not softened, because the row's whole claim is that this commit is the change that
+was measured, and once the surrounding code moved that claim needs a person.
